I’ll first inspect the pattern metadata, the frozen P0 workflow, and the available GSAS-II/search utilities. Then I’ll build a chemically plausible phase inventory, fit it residual-first, and verify that the exported CIFs, fractions, profile, and `final.gpx` agree exactly.

The context is a 900 °C SrCO₃ + WO₃ reaction, so SrWO₄ is the leading product, but carbonate/oxide carryover and Sr–W–O intermediates remain possible. I’m now extracting peak positions and checking the frozen COD index for all physically plausible Sr–W–O/C–O phases before committing to that assignment.

Peak-family matching already establishes ambient scheelite SrWO₄ as the dominant phase: its isolated peaks track the same angle-dependent displacement from 18° through 95°. A monoclinic WO₃ family explains the 23.45/23.97/24.73° triplet and several weaker high-angle peaks. One strong residual family near 29.75° is not yet assigned, so I’m keeping carbonate, Sr–W–O intermediates, and alternate WO₃ polymorphs on the watch list rather than forcing a two-phase result.

The first two-phase refinement converges to roughly 87.5 wt% SrWO₄ and 12.5 wt% monoclinic WO₃, but its 17.3% Rwp and coherent residuals at 29.75, 32.56, 44.67, 48.57, 53.44, 56.97, and 67.04° prove the inventory is incomplete. I’m screening complete phase models against that entire residual family; I am also rejecting the current unconstrained negative instrumental U as physically inadmissible, not treating its lower Rwp as a valid baseline.

The residual phase is now identified coherently as orthorhombic Sr₂WO₅, for which the frozen COD contains no exact record. The allowed analogue search found Ba₂ReO₅ (Pnma, COD 1529756); after the chemically isovalent Ba→Sr and Re→W substitutions, one adjusted cell predicts not just the 29.75° maximum but the full residual family—including 14.99, 16.61, 20.65, 22.09, 26.23, 30.89, 32.56, 35.32, 44.67, 48.57, 56.97, 61.22, and 66.98°. I’m now physical-screening that substituted model and fitting its cell before any coordinate refinement.

The Sr₂WO₅ substitution is geometrically sound after cell adjustment: W–O = 1.75–2.01 Å, shortest Sr–O = 2.60 Å, and shortest O–O = 2.52 Å, with no occupancy or hard-contact issue. Adding it drops Rwp from 17.3% to 9.15% and gives provisional fractions near 65.3% SrWO₄, 9.2% WO₃, and 25.5% Sr₂WO₅. I’m now separating crystallite-size broadening from strain and the common instrument profile; those fractions are not final until that physically constrained comparison reconverges.

Size-only broadening is stable and positive (about 0.08–0.12 µm), whereas strain-only drives all three microstrains negative; I’m rejecting that strain-only result as a width-cancellation artifact. The inventory and fractions are stable across these trials, but the common U/W/X fit is still pressing outside the physically allowed generator range. I’m therefore comparing the actual WO₃ polymorph records before constraining the shared profile, since the wrong WO₃ coordinates or symmetry can otherwise be absorbed spuriously into profile terms.

The re-challenged watch list is now clean: trigonal SrCO₃ and SrO refine to zero scale; Sr₃W₂O₉ remains below 0.2 wt% without unique support; orthorhombic SrCO₃ only survives by collapsing to an impossible 0.001 µm size and then destabilizing its cell, so it is a background/overlap surrogate. I’m retaining exactly three phases and moving to symmetry-constrained coordinate refinement, one phase at a time.

The three-phase inventory is now stable, and the remaining mismatch is mainly within peak shapes and relative intensities rather than unexplained reflections. I’m continuing with restrained coordinate and common-profile tests, then I’ll replay and export the exact final project so every submitted artifact is numerically consistent.

Coordinate freedom improves the fit substantially, but the first pass exposed strong parameter correlations and frozen variables. I’m screening those refinements geometrically now; any coordinate set that wins only by producing implausible bonds or unstable shifts will be discarded before nuisance terms are considered.

The whole-pattern angular trends support a real low-angle surface deficit plus a refined polarization factor; together they reduce Rwp from 9.43% to 7.67% with physically allowed values. The asymmetry term also settles near 0.016. I’m now testing phase-common displacement factors and texture axes, one phase at a time, because those can otherwise masquerade as coordinate shifts or composition changes.

The displacement-factor scan is behaving sensibly but confirms a strong covariance with polarization and scale. For SrWO₄ the physical lower edge, \(U_\mathrm{iso}=0.003\ \text{Å}^2\), is preferred; I’ll treat that boundary result cautiously and report the corresponding fraction sensitivity rather than implying unrealistically high precision.

No texture model has been accepted yet. The active test compares the permitted crystallographic axes independently for each phase, with the March–Dollase ratio bounded to 0.7–1.4; acceptance will require a stable improvement across several isolated reflection families, not merely a lower global residual.

The bounded-coordinate tests locate chemically sensible directions, but several coordinates press against the deliberately tight trust region; unconstrained minor-phase coordinates overfit and are being rejected. I’m replacing those hard edges with bond-geometry restraints and increasing their statistical weight until the refined models preserve the parent coordination environment.

The retained coordinate model now passes the geometry screen: SrWO₄ has W–O 1.85 Å, WO₃ retains its published distorted octahedra, and the substituted Sr₂WO₅ prototype has W–O ≥1.76 Å and Sr–O ≥2.57 Å. The unconstrained alternatives with 1.4–1.6 Å W–O contacts were rejected. I’m fixing the accepted coordinates, pruning the bound variables, and doing the required full-range reopen/replay before export.

The final project now replays natively with zero evaluator review flags, the three exported CIFs match the project atom-for-atom, and the project-derived fractions match `result.json` exactly. I’m doing one last artifact-level audit of file coverage, normalization, and the observed/calculated grid before handing it over.

Analysis complete. The modeled crystalline phases are:

| Phase | Weight fraction |
|---|---:|
| SrWO₄, I4₁/a | 62.38% |
| WO₃, P2₁/c | 9.72% |
| Sr₂WO₅, Pnma | 27.90% |

Fit: Rwp 6.863%, Rp 5.254%, goodness-of-fit 1.944. Native GSAS-II replay passed over all 8,282 points with no physical-review flags.

